{"type":"thinking","text":"I'm weighing how to get the voice ID: I could list the available voices through the asset search and pick one, but that wouldn't match the others. Reading the audio won't reveal the ID, and I can't listen to the voices to judge them. The default voice may be the first one listed, so I'll check the list with the HeyGen key.

I could splice only the changed phrase rather than regenerating whole scenes, but that still needs a matching voice. I'll list the available voices with the HeyGen key, looking for a clone or a "your voice" option, and I'll check the default count of 20.

"}

{"type":"thinking","text":"I'm looking at a male voice with a median pitch of 113 Hz and a conversational delivery, but I can't tell which voice it is. I'm weighing whether to generate a short test clip with a couple of candidates, but that's a costly guess, and asking would block the single change I was told to apply. Without knowing the voice ID, regenerating could produce an inconsistent voice, so I'm considering a likely default instead, though ideally there's a record to check.

I'm thinking about checking the tool's reference files, but I'll leave those alone since they're off limits. Instead I'm going to test the voices directly: generate the same short line in a few male candidates (Lewis, Brodie, Roderic, Braith, Lachie, Wade) and compare their word timings against the original vocals, since each voice has its own duration pattern. I'll run those generations in parallel and delete the test files afterward.

"}
